# Terraform Best Practices for Azure Infrastructure

## Code Organization and Structure

### File Structure
```
terraform/
├── main.tf              # Main configuration
├── variables.tf         # Input variables
├── outputs.tf           # Output values
├── terraform.tfvars     # Variable values
├── versions.tf          # Provider and Terraform versions
├── modules/             # Reusable modules
│   ├── resource_group/
│   ├── function_app/
│   ├── storage_account/
│   └── key_vault/
└── environments/        # Environment-specific configurations
    ├── dev/
    ├── tst/
    └── prd/
```

### Module Structure
```
module-name/
├── main.tf              # Main module configuration
├── variables.tf         # Module input variables
├── outputs.tf           # Module outputs
├── versions.tf          # Module-specific versions
└── README.md            # Module documentation
```

## Naming Conventions

### Resource Names
- Use lowercase letters and hyphens
- Follow Azure naming standards
- Include environment and region identifiers
- Example: `rg-twdemo-mcips-dev-twn-functions`

### Variable Names
- Use snake_case for variable names
- Be descriptive and clear
- Example: `resource_group_name`, `function_app_name`

### Output Names
- Use snake_case for output names
- Be descriptive of what the output represents
- Example: `function_app_url`, `storage_account_key`

## Variable Management

### Variable Definitions
```hcl
variable "environment" {
  description = "Environment (dev, tst, prd)"
  type        = string
  default     = "dev"
  validation {
    condition     = contains(["dev", "tst", "prd"], var.environment)
    error_message = "Environment must be one of: dev, tst, prd."
  }
}
```

### Variable Validation
- Always validate input variables
- Use meaningful error messages
- Validate against allowed values
- Check string lengths and formats

### Local Values
```hcl
locals {
  # Compute resource names based on naming standard
  resource_group_name = "rg-${var.application}-${var.cips}-${var.environment}-${var.region}-${var.description}"
  
  # Standard tags
  standard_tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
    Project     = "Taiwan Demo Functions"
    CIPS        = var.cips
    Application = var.application
    Region      = var.region
    Owner       = "Cloud Team"
    CostCenter  = "IT-001"
  }
}
```

## Resource Configuration

### Resource Naming
- Use consistent naming patterns
- Include environment and region
- Follow Azure naming conventions
- Example: `azurerm_resource_group.main`

### Resource Tags
- Apply standard tags to all resources
- Use consistent tag structure
- Include cost center and owner information
- Example:
```hcl
tags = merge(local.standard_tags, {
  Component = "function-app"
  Version   = "1.0.0"
})
```

### Data Sources
- Use data sources for existing resources
- Validate data source existence
- Handle missing resources gracefully
```hcl
data "azurerm_resource_group" "existing" {
  name = var.resource_group_name
}
```

## Module Design

### Module Inputs
- Define clear input variables
- Use appropriate variable types
- Provide meaningful defaults
- Include validation rules

### Module Outputs
- Export only necessary values
- Use descriptive output names
- Document output purposes
- Example:
```hcl
output "function_app_url" {
  description = "URL of the deployed Function App"
  value       = azurerm_function_app.main.default_hostname
}
```

### Module Dependencies
- Use explicit dependencies with `depends_on`
- Avoid circular dependencies
- Use data sources for cross-module references

## Security Best Practices

### Sensitive Data
- Use `sensitive = true` for sensitive variables
- Never hardcode secrets in Terraform files
- Use Azure Key Vault for secret management
- Example:
```hcl
variable "api_key" {
  description = "API key for external service"
  type        = string
  sensitive   = true
}
```

### Access Control
- Use least privilege principle
- Implement proper IAM roles
- Use managed identities where possible
- Example:
```hcl
resource "azurerm_user_assigned_identity" "function_identity" {
  name                = "id-${var.application}-${var.cips}-${var.environment}-funcaccess"
  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.main.name
  location            = azurerm_resource_group.main.location
}
```

## State Management

### Backend Configuration
- Use remote state storage
- Enable state locking
- Use separate state files per environment
- Example:
```hcl
terraform {
  backend "azurerm" {
    resource_group_name  = "terraform-state-rg"
    storage_account_name = "tfstatetwdemo"
    container_name       = "tfstate"
    key                  = "dev.terraform.tfstate"
  }
}
```

### State Security
- Enable encryption at rest
- Use access controls on state storage
- Regularly backup state files
- Use state locking to prevent conflicts

## Error Handling and Validation

### Resource Validation
- Validate resource configurations
- Use conditional creation with `count`
- Handle optional resources gracefully
- Example:
```hcl
resource "azurerm_key_vault" "main" {
  count = var.enable_key_vault ? 1 : 0
  
  name                = var.key_vault_name
  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.main.name
  location            = azurerm_resource_group.main.location
  # ... other configuration
}
```

### Error Messages
- Provide clear error messages
- Use validation blocks
- Include troubleshooting information
- Example:
```hcl
validation {
  condition     = length(var.storage_account_name) <= 24
  error_message = "Storage account name must be 24 characters or less."
}
```

## Performance Optimization

### Resource Dependencies
- Minimize unnecessary dependencies
- Use `depends_on` sparingly
- Parallelize resource creation where possible
- Use data sources for existing resources

### Module Optimization
- Keep modules focused and small
- Avoid deep module nesting
- Use consistent patterns across modules
- Reuse common configurations

## Documentation

### Code Comments
- Add comments for complex logic
- Document business requirements
- Explain resource relationships
- Use consistent comment style

### README Files
- Document module purpose and usage
- Include example configurations
- List required variables and outputs
- Provide deployment instructions

## Testing and Validation

### Plan Validation
- Always run `terraform plan` before apply
- Review planned changes carefully
- Validate resource configurations
- Check for potential issues

### Code Quality
- Use `terraform fmt` for formatting
- Run `terraform validate` for syntax checking
- Use `tflint` for additional validation
- Follow consistent coding style

## Deployment Strategy

### Environment Separation
- Use separate configurations per environment
- Maintain consistent naming across environments
- Use environment-specific variable files
- Example: `dev.tfvars`, `tst.tfvars`, `prd.tfvars`

### Deployment Process
- Use CI/CD pipelines for deployments
- Implement approval gates for production
- Use workspace separation for environments
- Monitor deployment logs and metrics

## Monitoring and Maintenance

### Resource Monitoring
- Enable diagnostic settings
- Configure logging and metrics
- Set up alerts for critical resources
- Monitor resource costs and usage

### Maintenance Tasks
- Regularly update provider versions
- Review and update resource configurations
- Clean up unused resources
- Monitor for deprecated features
